# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 have "done_without_involuntarily" and "hero_sacrifices_for_something"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 has "◇without_happiness" and "done_without_involuntarily" and "done_without_involuntarily → without_happiness" and "hero_sacrifices_for_something"; they differ when only "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "the hero or martyr gives up his own happiness voluntarily, for the sake of something he prizes more" are true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "the hero or martyr gives up his own happiness voluntarily, for the sake of something he prizes more" are true and "people do without happiness" and "possibly, people do without happiness" are false. Is S2, "Unquestionably it is possible to do without happiness; it is done involuntarily by nineteen-twentieths of mankind, even in those parts of our present world which are least deep in barbarism; and it often has to be done voluntarily by the hero or the martyr, for the sake of something which he prizes more than his individual happiness.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 have "sacrifice_has_end ∧ ¬sacrifice_own_end"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 has "□sacrifice_has_end ∧ ¬sacrifice_own_end"; they differ when only "self-sacrifice must be for some end" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "must" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "self-sacrifice must be for some end" is true and "self-sacrifice is its own end" and "necessarily, self-sacrifice must be for some end" are false. Is S4, "It is noble to be capable of resigning entirely one's own portion of happiness, or chances of it: but, after all, this self-sacrifice must be for some end; it is not its own end; and if we are told that its end is not happiness, but virtue, which is better than happiness, I ask, would the sacrifice be made if the hero or martyr did not believe that it would earn for others immunity from similar sacrifices?", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 have "done_without_involuntarily → without_happiness" and "¬admirable_ascetic → ¬O ascetic_renunciation"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 has "O ascetic_renunciation → admirable_ascetic"; they differ when only "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "renouncing happiness for some other purpose deserves admiration" are true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 have "¬O ascetic_renunciation"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 has "◇ascetic_renunciation ∧ ¬O ascetic_renunciation"; they differ when only "it ought to be that men renounce happiness for a purpose other than increasing happiness" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 as what ought to be or is permitted; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 as both: its "may" or "can" or "should" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "it ought to be that men renounce happiness for a purpose other than increasing happiness" and "it ought to be that men renounce happiness for a purpose other than increasing happiness" are true and "possibly, men renounce happiness for a purpose other than increasing happiness" and "it ought to be that men renounce happiness for a purpose other than increasing happiness" are false. Is the conclusion, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/negation: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 P2 (S2): the sentence says "without", but P2 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the hero or martyr gives up his own happiness voluntarily, for the sake of something he prizes more"
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/negation: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 P2 (S2): the sentence says "without", but P2 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the hero or martyr gives up his own happiness voluntarily, for the sake of something he prizes more"
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/negation: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 P4 (S2): the sentence says "without", but P4 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the hero or martyr gives up his own happiness voluntarily, for the sake of something he prizes more"
